  "worked_solution": "The position function of a particle moving in a circular path of radius $a$ in the $xy$-plane with a constant angular velocity $\\omega $ is given by\n\n$$\nr(t)=a(\\operatorname{cos}\u2061\\omega t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u2061\\omega t\\,j),\n$$\n\nwhere it is assumed that the circle is centered at $O,$ and the particle is at the position $(a,0)$ when $t=0.$\n\nIn our case, we have $a=5$ and $\\omega =4\\text{\u00a0rad/s},$ so we get that the position function is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r(t) & =5(\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t\\,j).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe find the velocity and acceleration vectors by differentiating. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v(t) & =(5(\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t\\,i+\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t\\,j))^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-20\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t\\,j \\\\ a(t) & =(-20\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t\\,i+20\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t\\,j)^{\u2032} \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}\u20614t\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}\u20614t\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we find the acceleration vector at $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}a(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-80\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(4\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\operatorname{cos}\u2061(2\\pi )\\,i-80\\operatorname{sin}\u2061(2\\pi )\\,j \\\\  & =-80\\,i\\end{aligned}\n$$",
